<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banias</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Paneas, Caesarea Philippi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caesarea Philippi was an ancient city at the springs of the Jordan below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>, on the site of the shrine of Pan at Paneas. Herod's son Philip the Tetrarch rebuilt it around 2 BCE and made it his capital, naming it after Augustus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Agrippa II</a> later renamed it Neronias, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> place Peter's confession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> near it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banias.jpg" alt="Banias" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: gugganij · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banias_Spring_Cliff_Pan%27s_Cave.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banias lies at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>, beside the spring that feeds the Banias River, one of the main tributaries of the Jordan. The spring once gushed from a large cave in a cliff, until an earthquake moved it to the foot of the terrace below. Before Greek influence its god was called Ba'al-gad or Ba'al Hermon; the Ptolemaic kings built a cult centre there in the 3rd century BCE, and the cave was dedicated to Pan. The name Paneas, which became Baniyas in Arabic, comes from Pan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> defeated the Ptolemaic army near the place at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-panium">Battle of Panium</a> around 200–198 BCE. After 20 BCE the district passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>. Agrippa II renamed the city Neronias in 61 CE, a name dropped in 68 CE, and Vespasian visited it in 67 CE. Excavations show severe decline in the early Byzantine period, which scholars link to heavy taxation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms. In 635 the city surrendered on favourable terms to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, and it remained the chief town of the district of al-Djawlan.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, who called it Belinas, held it in 1129–1132 and 1140–1164, when it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-ii-of-toron">Humphrey II of Toron</a>. Nur ad-Din besieged it in 1157 and captured it on 18 November 1164. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa">Al-Mu'azzam Isa</a> had its fortress destroyed in March 1219. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> period it was the seat of a subdistrict under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. A traveller counted about 500 inhabitants in 1825.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1923 the border between French Syria and British Palestine was set 750 metres south of the spring. The Golani Brigade captured the village on 10 June 1967, the last day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>; its residents fled to Majdal Shams, and Israeli bulldozers destroyed the village, leaving the mosque, church and shrines. The site, in the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights, has been the Hermon Stream (Banias) Nature Reserve since 1977, and Christians come on pilgrimage to the place the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> name Caesarea Philippi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Agrippa II received Chalcis and from 53 the northern lands, renaming <b>Caesarea Philippi</b> as Neronias.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship controlled hill villages and the routes from Tyre to <b>Banyas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-toron">Lordship of Toron</a></li><li><blockquote>He refounded <b>Paneas</b> at the springs of the Jordan as Caesarea Philippi and raised Bethsaida to a city named Julias.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
